LARGEST ISLAND (Flip One 0)
🔹 Problem Statement

You are given an n x n binary matrix grid containing only 0s and 1s.

You are allowed to change at most one 0 to 1.

Return the size of the largest possible island after the flip.

An island is formed by connecting adjacent 1s (4-directionally).

🔹 Example 1
Input:
[
 [1, 0],
 [0, 1]
]

Output:

3

Explanation:
Flip one 0 → Connect three 1s.

🔹 Example 2
Input:
[
 [1, 1],
 [1, 0]
]

Output:

4

Flip bottom-right 0 to 1 → entire grid becomes one island.

🔹 Brute Force (Bad Approach)

For every 0:

Flip it

Run DFS to calculate island size

Revert

Time Complexity:

O(n² * n²) = O(n^4) ❌

Too slow.

🔹 Optimal Approach (Label Islands First 🔥)

We solve in 2 phases.

🟢 Phase 1: Label Each Island

Instead of keeping 1’s as 1,
we mark each island with a unique id (starting from 2).

Why start at 2?
Because 0 and 1 already exist.

Example:

Before:
1 1 0
0 1 0
1 0 1

After:
2 2 0
0 2 0
3 0 4

We also store island sizes:

Map:
2 → 3
3 → 1
4 → 1
🟢 Phase 2: Try Flipping Each 0

For each cell = 0:

Check 4 neighbors.

Collect unique island ids.

Sum their sizes + 1 (for flipped cell).

Take maximum.

🔹 Complete Algorithm
1. Traverse grid
2. If cell == 1:
      Do DFS
      Mark island with new id
      Store island size in map

3. result = max island size

4. For each cell == 0:
      Check 4 neighbors
      Add unique island areas
      result = max(result, sum + 1)

5. Return result
🔹 Java Code (Optimized)
import java.util.*;

class LargestIsland {

    int[][] grid;
    int n;
    int id = 2;
    Map<Integer, Integer> areaMap = new HashMap<>();

    public int largestIsland(int[][] grid) {
        this.grid = grid;
        n = grid.length;

        // Phase 1: Label islands
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    int area = dfs(i, j, id);
                    areaMap.put(id, area);
                    id++;
                }
            }
        }

        int max = 0;
        for (int value : areaMap.values()) {
            max = Math.max(max, value);
        }

        // Phase 2: Flip each 0
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (grid[i][j] == 0) {
                    max = Math.max(max, connect(i, j));
                }
            }
        }

        return max == 0 ? n * n : max;
    }

    // DFS to mark island
    private int dfs(int r, int c, int id) {

        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= n || c >= n || grid[r][c] != 1)
            return 0;

        grid[r][c] = id;

        return 1
            + dfs(r + 1, c, id)
            + dfs(r - 1, c, id)
            + dfs(r, c + 1, id)
            + dfs(r, c - 1, id);
    }

    // Calculate connected area after flip
    private int connect(int r, int c) {

        Set<Integer> visited = new HashSet<>();
        int sum = 1;

        int[][] directions = {{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}};

        for (int[] dir : directions) {

            int nr = r + dir[0];
            int nc = c + dir[1];

            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < n && nc < n) {

                int neighborId = grid[nr][nc];

                if (neighborId > 1 && visited.add(neighborId)) {
                    sum += areaMap.get(neighborId);
                }
            }
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
